1
00:00:00,024 --> 00:00:05,284
On September 18th, 2009, a television show
ended after seventy-two years on the air.

2
00:00:06,334 --> 00:00:07,914
Almost nobody noticed.

3
00:00:08,914 --> 00:00:11,783
The question here is not why
a soap opera got canceled.

4
00:00:12,254 --> 00:00:14,373
Soap operas get canceled constantly.

5
00:00:15,424 --> 00:00:19,124
The real question is how the single
longest-running scripted program in the

6
00:00:19,124 --> 00:00:24,014
history of broadcasting could vanish
without the kind of send-off television

7
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:26,003
usually reserves for something that old.

8
00:00:27,383 --> 00:00:31,313
The answer says less about this one
show than it does about how quietly

9
00:00:31,313 --> 00:00:33,903
most of television actually disappears.

10
00:00:34,994 --> 00:00:39,363
The story starts in 1937 with
a woman named Irna Phillips.

11
00:00:40,413 --> 00:00:43,103
Two years earlier, Phillips
had given birth to a stillborn

12
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:44,634
child at nineteen years old.

13
00:00:45,303 --> 00:00:48,163
In the aftermath, she found herself
listening to the sermons of a

14
00:00:48,163 --> 00:00:51,584
preacher whose church centered
on the brotherhood of man.

15
00:00:52,553 --> 00:00:56,423
Those broadcasts gave her something to
hold onto, and that feeling became the

16
00:00:56,424 --> 00:01:02,002
seed of a new kind of radio program
She built a 15-minute serial around a

17
00:01:02,002 --> 00:01:07,012
widowed minister named Reverend John
Ruthledge, a man neighbors turned

18
00:01:07,012 --> 00:01:08,472
to when their lives went sideways.

19
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:15,011
In his study sat a lamp, always lit, meant
to represent the small guidance he offered

20
00:01:15,041 --> 00:01:16,702
anyone who walked through his door.

21
00:01:17,871 --> 00:01:20,351
Phillips named the show after that lamp.

22
00:01:21,632 --> 00:01:27,932
It debuted on NBC Radio on January
25th, 1937, and almost nobody

23
00:01:27,932 --> 00:01:30,441
involved could have guessed
what it was actually starting.

24
00:01:30,861 --> 00:01:34,431
Phillips herself is barely remembered
outside television history books

25
00:01:34,851 --> 00:01:38,581
despite essentially inventing the
format this entire genre still runs on.

26
00:01:39,061 --> 00:01:42,341
She went on to create another
long-running serial that actually stayed

27
00:01:42,342 --> 00:01:47,071
on the air one full year longer than
this one did, closing out in 2010.

28
00:01:47,861 --> 00:01:52,581
But even that show fell more than a decade
short of this one's total run because

29
00:01:52,592 --> 00:01:57,641
nothing else Phillips built carried the
15-year radio head start this one had.

30
00:01:59,081 --> 00:02:03,051
Along the way, she pioneered techniques
other writers borrowed for decades:

31
00:02:03,572 --> 00:02:08,181
cliffhangers timed to the end of every
episode and storylines that unfolded over

32
00:02:08,181 --> 00:02:10,401
months instead of wrapping up cleanly.

33
00:02:11,781 --> 00:02:15,922
Everything that makes a soap opera
recognizably a soap opera traces back to

34
00:02:15,922 --> 00:02:18,021
decisions she made building this one show.

35
00:02:19,401 --> 00:02:27,362
The show moved to CBS Radio in 1947, and
then on June 30th, 1952, it made the jump

36
00:02:27,362 --> 00:02:32,791
to CBS Television, running in both formats
side by side for a few overlapping years

37
00:02:32,791 --> 00:02:35,191
before radio quietly faded out entirely.

38
00:02:35,621 --> 00:02:39,751
From that first radio broadcast to its
final television episode, the show stayed

39
00:02:39,751 --> 00:02:42,062
on the air for 72 consecutive years.

40
00:02:43,482 --> 00:02:46,781
On television alone, it
produced fifteen thousand seven

41
00:02:46,781 --> 00:02:48,361
hundred and sixty-two episodes.

42
00:02:49,421 --> 00:02:53,471
Counting the radio years, the total climbs
past eighteen thousand five hundred.

43
00:02:54,921 --> 00:02:58,531
Guinness World Records eventually
recognized it as the longest-running

44
00:02:58,531 --> 00:03:02,171
scripted drama in the history
of broadcasting, a title no

45
00:03:02,171 --> 00:03:05,911
other program in any format has
come close to matching since.

46
00:03:07,374 --> 00:03:11,084
To put that number in perspective,
a show airing five days a week for

47
00:03:11,084 --> 00:03:14,274
fifty-two weeks a year would need to
run for more than sixty years just

48
00:03:14,274 --> 00:03:16,374
to reach fifteen thousand episodes.

49
00:03:17,053 --> 00:03:20,253
This show did that and then kept
going for over a decade more.

50
00:03:20,994 --> 00:03:24,693
And unlike a weekly drama that gets
roughly twenty-two episodes a year to

51
00:03:24,694 --> 00:03:28,964
work with, a daily serial like this
one produced that many episodes in

52
00:03:28,974 --> 00:03:32,904
about a month, which meant the cast was
effectively shooting an entire season's

53
00:03:32,904 --> 00:03:37,973
worth of story every four weeks, year
after year, for seven decades straight.

54
00:03:38,543 --> 00:03:42,734
A production running at that pace
does not just accumulate episodes, it

55
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:46,883
accumulates people, and this is where
the story turns genuinely strange.

56
00:03:47,284 --> 00:03:51,823
Long before he was a movie star,
Kevin Bacon spent nineteen eighty and

57
00:03:51,824 --> 00:03:56,223
nineteen eighty-one playing a teenage
alcoholic named Tim Werner on this

58
00:03:56,223 --> 00:04:00,773
show, years before Diner put him on
the map and Footloose made him famous.

59
00:04:01,273 --> 00:04:05,593
In nineteen eighty-nine, a young actress
took a minor role as a babysitter for one

60
00:04:05,593 --> 00:04:07,713
of the show's most memorable characters.

61
00:04:08,513 --> 00:04:12,563
Her name was Calista Flockhart,
eight years before Ally McBeal

62
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:13,833
made her a household name.

63
00:04:14,583 --> 00:04:16,793
James Earl Jones passed through its cast.

64
00:04:17,263 --> 00:04:22,923
So did Cicely Tyson, Allison Janney,
and a teenage Hayden Panettiere.

65
00:04:22,923 --> 00:04:25,213
None of them were famous yet
when this show hired them.

66
00:04:25,923 --> 00:04:29,413
Most of them would not have been
hireable anywhere else because daytime

67
00:04:29,414 --> 00:04:33,294
drama was one of the only places a
young or unproven actor could work

68
00:04:33,294 --> 00:04:37,703
steadily while learning the job in
front of a live audience of millions.

69
00:04:39,114 --> 00:04:42,464
That is the part almost nobody
connects to a single title.

70
00:04:43,203 --> 00:04:46,833
An entertainment institution operating
quietly in the middle of the afternoon

71
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:51,893
for seven decades, training a meaningful
share of Hollywood's future household

72
00:04:51,893 --> 00:04:56,063
names before anyone outside daytime
television was paying attention, while

73
00:04:56,063 --> 00:05:00,364
also quietly teaching primetime how to
keep an audience hooked across years

74
00:05:00,364 --> 00:05:06,393
instead of episodes, a trick shows like
Dallas and Dynasty borrowed wholesale once

75
00:05:06,393 --> 00:05:11,224
networks realized daytime's cliffhanger
formula worked just as well after dark.

76
00:05:12,123 --> 00:05:15,313
Not everyone on the show was passing
through on their way somewhere else.

77
00:05:15,743 --> 00:05:20,383
One actress, Kim Zimmer, played the same
character, a woman named Reva Shayne,

78
00:05:20,813 --> 00:05:25,013
across nearly two thousand episodes
spanning more than two decades, leaving

79
00:05:25,023 --> 00:05:28,603
and returning to the role twice before
playing her one final time in the very

80
00:05:28,603 --> 00:05:30,594
last episode the show ever produced.

81
00:05:31,034 --> 00:05:35,453
Four decades into the show's run, viewers
were still watching a character built in

82
00:05:35,453 --> 00:05:39,913
the nineteen eighties carry the finale of
a series that started before television

83
00:05:39,913 --> 00:05:41,863
itself existed as a mass medium.

84
00:05:42,443 --> 00:05:46,753
And then in April of two thousand
and nine, CBS canceled it.

85
00:05:47,763 --> 00:05:50,404
The reasoning was almost
aggressively unglamorous.

86
00:05:50,984 --> 00:05:53,903
By that point, the show had become
the lowest rated of the eight

87
00:05:53,914 --> 00:05:58,103
daytime dramas still airing anywhere
on American network television.

88
00:05:59,344 --> 00:06:03,603
It was averaging two point one seven
million viewers with a zero point

89
00:06:03,613 --> 00:06:07,533
nine rating among the women eighteen
to forty-nine that advertisers

90
00:06:07,533 --> 00:06:09,123
were actually paying to reach.

91
00:06:10,234 --> 00:06:14,303
Both numbers were sliding hard, down
eighteen percent in total viewers

92
00:06:14,304 --> 00:06:18,643
and twenty-five percent in that core
demographic compared to the season before.

93
00:06:19,844 --> 00:06:22,783
Underneath the ratings sat a
broader shift that had nothing

94
00:06:22,783 --> 00:06:24,083
to do with the show's quality.

95
00:06:24,603 --> 00:06:28,233
The audience daytime dramas were built
for, women reliably at home in the

96
00:06:28,243 --> 00:06:31,993
middle of the day, had been shrinking
for years as more women worked outside

97
00:06:31,993 --> 00:06:34,313
the house and found community elsewhere.

98
00:06:35,734 --> 00:06:40,884
CBS was also simply looking for a cheaper
hour of programming to fill the same slot.

99
00:06:41,853 --> 00:06:45,054
A seventy-two-year run ended
for essentially the same reasons

100
00:06:45,054 --> 00:06:48,043
a struggling show half its age
gets canceled every season.

101
00:06:49,483 --> 00:06:53,003
Production wrapped its final scenes on
August eleventh, two thousand and nine.

102
00:06:53,973 --> 00:06:58,064
The finale aired five weeks later
on September eighteenth, closing

103
00:06:58,064 --> 00:07:00,774
with a one-year flash-forward that
gave the audience a glimpse of

104
00:07:00,774 --> 00:07:02,314
where its characters would end up.

105
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:06,040
There was no prime-time
special built around it.

106
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:10,200
No retrospective aired opposite
it on a competing network.

107
00:07:11,530 --> 00:07:15,019
Coverage existed, but nothing
close to what greets the ending

108
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:18,510
of a show people watch once a week
and plan their evening around.

109
00:07:19,919 --> 00:07:22,219
That gap is the actual turn in this story.

110
00:07:23,630 --> 00:07:27,810
The ending of the longest-running scripted
program in broadcast history barely

111
00:07:27,810 --> 00:07:31,989
registered outside the community that
had watched it for decades, while shows

112
00:07:31,989 --> 00:07:36,220
a fraction of its age and total output
get farewell tours that last for months.

113
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,800
That is not a contradiction.

114
00:07:39,189 --> 00:07:41,549
It is exactly how a habit disappears.

115
00:07:42,479 --> 00:07:46,419
A daily program does not build the kind
of anticipation a weekly event does

116
00:07:46,799 --> 00:07:50,520
because nobody spends a week looking
forward to Tuesday afternoon the way

117
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:52,369
they look forward to a season finale.

118
00:07:53,729 --> 00:07:57,450
Something people watched every single
day for seventy-two years left behind

119
00:07:57,459 --> 00:08:01,999
less cultural memory than a single
viral season of a show that lasted six.

120
00:08:02,739 --> 00:08:05,429
And this show was not the
last one to go quietly.

121
00:08:06,669 --> 00:08:10,629
Within three years, three more of the
eight daytime dramas still airing when it

122
00:08:10,639 --> 00:08:15,659
ended had also been canceled, one after
another for close to identical reasons:

123
00:08:16,169 --> 00:08:21,070
shrinking ratings, an aging audience,
and networks unwilling to keep funding an

124
00:08:21,070 --> 00:08:23,450
expensive daily hour of scripted drama.

125
00:08:24,879 --> 00:08:28,340
By the middle of the 2010s, only
three network daytime soaps were

126
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:32,099
left standing in the entire country,
down from more than a dozen at

127
00:08:32,099 --> 00:08:34,079
the genre's peak decades earlier.

128
00:08:35,510 --> 00:08:39,379
This show was not an isolated case
of one program losing its footing.

129
00:08:40,289 --> 00:08:44,080
It was the biggest domino in a collapse
that had already started, and its

130
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:47,989
sheer size was the only thing that made
its fall worth remarking on at all.

131
00:08:49,019 --> 00:08:51,499
None of that erases what
actually happened here.

132
00:08:52,639 --> 00:08:57,170
A woman processing a personal loss built
a small radio drama around a lamp in

133
00:08:57,170 --> 00:09:01,309
a minister's study, and it grew into
a machine that ran for seventy-two

134
00:09:01,309 --> 00:09:05,749
straight years, produced more episodes
than almost anything else broadcast in

135
00:09:05,749 --> 00:09:10,469
that time, quietly wrote the rulebook
the entire soap genre and half of

136
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:14,559
primetime drama still follows, and
trained a startling number of the actors

137
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:18,709
who would go on to define American film
and television, all while most of the

138
00:09:18,709 --> 00:09:20,499
country never thought about it twice.

139
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:26,130
Today, only a handful of daytime dramas
remain on the air at all, and none of them

140
00:09:26,130 --> 00:09:30,000
will realistically live long enough to
challenge the record this one still holds.

141
00:09:31,370 --> 00:09:35,439
The longest story television ever
told did not end with a bang,

142
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:37,540
and it never really needed one.

143
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:42,209
It ended the way it had survived
every single year before that one.

144
00:09:43,049 --> 00:09:46,930
Another Tuesday, another quiet
episode, until there simply was

145
00:09:46,930 --> 00:09:48,329
not another one left to make.
